# Rust Performance Optimization

Rust enables writing high-performance code that rivals C and C++ while maintaining memory safety. These guidelines will help you write efficient Rust code that makes the best use of system resources.

## Use zero-cost abstractions

- **Leverage Rust's zero-cost abstractions** wherever possible

  ```rust
  // Zero-cost iterators - compiles to efficient loops
  fn sum_array(data: &[i32]) -> i32 {
      // This is as fast as a hand-written loop
      data.iter().sum()
  }

  // Zero-cost generics - no runtime overhead
  fn min<T: Ord>(a: T, b: T) -> T {
      if a <= b { a } else { b }
  }

  // Traits with static dispatch - resolved at compile time
  trait Shape {
      fn area(&self) -> f64;
  }

  impl Shape for Circle {
      fn area(&self) -> f64 {
          std::f64::consts::PI * self.radius * self.radius
      }
  }

  // Compile-time function evaluation
  const fn factorial(n: u64) -> u64 {
      match n {
          0 | 1 => 1,
          n => n * factorial(n - 1),
      }
  }

  const LOOKUP_TABLE: [u64; 10] = {
      let mut table = [0; 10];
      let mut i = 0;
      while i < 10 {
          table[i] = factorial(i);
          i += 1;
      }
      table
  };
  ```

- **Avoid unnecessary boxing or dynamic dispatch** when static dispatch is sufficient

  ```rust
  // Slower: Dynamic dispatch with trait objects
  fn process_shapes_dynamic(shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>>) -> f64 {
      shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum()
  }

  // Faster: Static dispatch with generics
  fn process_shapes_static<T: Shape>(shapes: &[T]) -> f64 {
      shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum()
  }

  // Alternative: Enum-based approach for heterogeneous collections
  enum ShapeEnum {
      Circle(Circle),
      Rectangle(Rectangle),
      Triangle(Triangle),
  }

  impl ShapeEnum {
      fn area(&self) -> f64 {
          match self {
              ShapeEnum::Circle(c) => c.area(),
              ShapeEnum::Rectangle(r) => r.area(),
              ShapeEnum::Triangle(t) => t.area(),
          }
      }
  }

  fn process_shapes_enum(shapes: &[ShapeEnum]) -> f64 {
      shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum()
  }
  ```

- **Use const generics** for compile-time optimized code

  ```rust
  // Use const generics for arrays of any size
  fn sum_array<const N: usize>(arr: &[i32; N]) -> i32 {
      let mut sum = 0;
      let mut i = 0;
      while i < N {
          sum += arr[i];
          i += 1;
      }
      sum
  }

  // Matrix implementation with const generics
  struct Matrix<T, const ROWS: usize, const COLS: usize> {
      data: [[T; COLS]; ROWS],
  }

  impl<T: Copy + Default, const ROWS: usize, const COLS: usize>
      Matrix<T, ROWS, COLS> {
      fn new() -> Self {
          Self {
              data: [[T::default(); COLS]; ROWS],
          }
      }
  }
  ```

## Optimize memory usage

- **Minimize allocation and use stack allocation** when possible

  ```rust
  // Bad: Unnecessary allocations
  fn process_string_slow(input: &str) -> String {
      let mut result = String::new();
      for c in input.chars() {
          let upper = c.to_uppercase().to_string(); // Allocation for each char
          result.push_str(&upper); // Potentially multiple allocations
      }
      result
  }

  // Better: Pre-allocate and minimize allocations
  fn process_string_fast(input: &str) -> String {
      // Estimate capacity to avoid reallocations
      let mut result = String::with_capacity(input.len() * 2);
      for c in input.chars() {
          for upper_c in c.to_uppercase() {
              result.push(upper_c); // No allocation per char
          }
      }
      result
  }

  // Use &str instead of String when possible
  fn find_substring(haystack: &str, needle: &str) -> bool {
      haystack.contains(needle)
  }

  // Use arrays for small fixed-size collections
  fn fixed_buffer() {
      let buffer: [u8; 1024] = [0; 1024]; // On stack, no allocation
      // Use buffer...
  }
  ```

- **Be mindful of struct layout and padding**

  ```rust
  // Inefficient: Struct with poor memory layout
  struct Inefficient {
      a: u8,    // 1 byte + 7 bytes padding
      b: u64,   // 8 bytes
      c: u8,    // 1 byte + 7 bytes padding
      d: u64,   // 8 bytes
  }  // Total: 32 bytes

  // Better: Reorder fields to minimize padding
  struct Better {
      b: u64,   // 8 bytes
      d: u64,   // 8 bytes
      a: u8,    // 1 byte
      c: u8,    // 1 byte + 6 bytes padding
  }  // Total: 24 bytes

  // Use explicit alignment/packing when needed
  #[repr(C)]
  struct ExplicitLayout {
      a: u32,
      b: u16,
      c: u8,
  }

  #[repr(packed)]
  struct Packed {
      a: u32,
      b: u16,
      c: u8,
  }

  // Use alignment to improve cache performance
  #[repr(align(64))]
  struct CacheAligned {
      data: [u8; 1024],
  }
  ```

- **Use specialized collection types** for small or stack-allocated collections

  ```rust
  use smallvec::{SmallVec, smallvec};

  // Uses stack for small collections, heap for larger ones
  fn process_items(items: &[u32]) -> SmallVec<[u32; 8]> {
      // Stores up to 8 elements on the stack, then uses heap
      let mut result: SmallVec<[u32; 8]> = smallvec![];

      for item in items {
          if *item % 2 == 0 {
              result.push(*item);
          }
      }

      result
  }

  use arrayvec::ArrayVec;

  // Strictly stack-allocated, fixed capacity
  fn top_k_items(items: &[u32], k: usize) -> ArrayVec<u32, 16> {
      let mut result = ArrayVec::<u32, 16>::new();

      for item in items.iter().take(k.min(16)) {
          result.push(*item);
      }

      result
  }
  ```

- **Consider using custom allocators** for specialized needs

  ```rust
  use std::alloc::{GlobalAlloc, Layout, System};

  // Simple custom allocator that tracks allocations
  struct TracingAllocator;

  unsafe impl GlobalAlloc for TracingAllocator {
      unsafe fn alloc(&self, layout: Layout) -> *mut u8 {
          println!("Allocating {} bytes", layout.size());
          System.alloc(layout)
      }

      unsafe fn dealloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) {
          println!("Deallocating {} bytes", layout.size());
          System.dealloc(ptr, layout)
      }
  }

  // Register the global allocator
  #[global_allocator]
  static ALLOCATOR: TracingAllocator = TracingAllocator;
  ```

## Employ efficient algorithms and data structures

- **Choose appropriate algorithms** with the right complexity

  ```rust
  // Bad: O(n²) algorithm for finding duplicates
  fn has_duplicates_slow(data: &[i32]) -> bool {
      for i in 0..data.len() {
          for j in i+1..data.len() {
              if data[i] == data[j] {
                  return true;
              }
          }
      }
      false
  }

  // Better: O(n log n) with sorting
  fn has_duplicates_medium(data: &[i32]) -> bool {
      if data.is_empty() {
          return false;
      }

      let mut sorted = data.to_vec();
      sorted.sort();

      for i in 0..sorted.len()-1 {
          if sorted[i] == sorted[i+1] {
              return true;
          }
      }

      false
  }

  // Best: O(n) with HashSet
  use std::collections::HashSet;

  fn has_duplicates_fast(data: &[i32]) -> bool {
      let mut seen = HashSet::with_capacity(data.len());

      for &item in data {
          if !seen.insert(item) {
              return true;
          }
      }

      false
  }
  ```

- **Use specialized data structures** for specific access patterns

  ```rust
  use std::collections::{HashMap, BTreeMap, HashSet, BTreeSet, VecDeque, BinaryHeap};

  // Fast lookups, unordered
  fn frequency_count(data: &[String]) -> HashMap<String, usize> {
      let mut counts = HashMap::new();
      for item in data {
          *counts.entry(item.clone()).or_insert(0) += 1;
      }
      counts
  }

  // Ordered map with slower lookups
  fn sorted_frequency_count(data: &[String]) -> BTreeMap<String, usize> {
      let mut counts = BTreeMap::new();
      for item in data {
          *counts.entry(item.clone()).or_insert(0) += 1;
      }
      counts
  }

  // Fast lookups for membership testing
  fn unique_items(data: &[String]) -> HashSet<String> {
      data.iter().cloned().collect()
  }

  // Efficient queue operations
  fn breadth_first_search(graph: &Graph, start: usize) -> Vec<usize> {
      let mut visited = vec![false; graph.size()];
      let mut queue = VecDeque::new();
      let mut result = Vec::new();

      visited[start] = true;
      queue.push_back(start);

      while let Some(node) = queue.pop_front() {
          result.push(node);

          for &neighbor in &graph.neighbors(node) {
              if !visited[neighbor] {
                  visited[neighbor] = true;
                  queue.push_back(neighbor);
              }
          }
      }

      result
  }

  // Priority queue for algorithms like Dijkstra
  fn k_smallest(data: &[i32], k: usize) -> Vec<i32> {
      let mut heap = BinaryHeap::new();

      for &item in data {
          heap.push(-item); // Negate for min-heap behavior
      }

      let mut result = Vec::with_capacity(k);
      for _ in 0..k.min(data.len()) {
          if let Some(item) = heap.pop() {
              result.push(-item); // Negate back
          }
      }

      result
  }
  ```

- **Consider cache locality** in data structure design

  ```rust
  // Poor cache locality: Array of structs
  struct Point3D {
      x: f32,
      y: f32,
      z: f32,
  }

  // Processing has poor cache locality
  fn calculate_distances_aos(points: &[Point3D], origin: &Point3D) -> Vec<f32> {
      points.iter()
          .map(|p| {
              let dx = p.x - origin.x;
              let dy = p.y - origin.y;
              let dz = p.z - origin.z;
              (dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz).sqrt()
          })
          .collect()
  }

  // Better cache locality: Struct of arrays
  struct Points3D {
      x: Vec<f32>,
      y: Vec<f32>,
      z: Vec<f32>,
  }

  impl Points3D {
      fn new(capacity: usize) -> Self {
          Self {
              x: Vec::with_capacity(capacity),
              y: Vec::with_capacity(capacity),
              z: Vec::with_capacity(capacity),
          }
      }

      fn push(&mut self, x: f32, y: f32, z: f32) {
          self.x.push(x);
          self.y.push(y);
          self.z.push(z);
      }

      fn len(&self) -> usize {
          self.x.len()
      }
  }

  // Processing has better cache locality
  fn calculate_distances_soa(points: &Points3D, origin: &Point3D) -> Vec<f32> {
      let mut distances = Vec::with_capacity(points.len());

      for i in 0..points.len() {
          let dx = points.x[i] - origin.x;
          let dy = points.y[i] - origin.y;
          let dz = points.z[i] - origin.z;
          distances.push((dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz).sqrt());
      }

      distances
  }
  ```

## Profile before optimizing

- **Use benchmarking tools like `criterion`** for accurate measurements

  ```rust
  // In Cargo.toml
  [dev-dependencies]
  criterion = "0.4"

  [[bench]]
  name = "my_benchmark"
  harness = false

  // In benches/my_benchmark.rs
  use criterion::{black_box, criterion_group, criterion_main, Criterion};

  fn fibonacci(n: u64) -> u64 {
      match n {
          0 => 0,
          1 => 1,
          n => fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2),
      }
  }

  fn memoized_fibonacci(n: u64) -> u64 {
      fn fib_memo(n: u64, memo: &mut [Option<u64>]) -> u64 {
          if let Some(value) = memo[n as usize] {
              return value;
          }

          let result = match n {
              0 => 0,
              1 => 1,
              n => fib_memo(n-1, memo) + fib_memo(n-2, memo),
          };

          memo[n as usize] = Some(result);
          result
      }

      let mut memo = vec![None; (n + 1) as usize];
      fib_memo(n, &mut memo)
  }

  fn criterion_benchmark(c: &mut Criterion) {
      c.bench_function("fibonacci 10", |b| {
          b.iter(|| fibonacci(black_box(10)))
      });

      c.bench_function("memoized_fibonacci 10", |b| {
          b.iter(|| memoized_fibonacci(black_box(10)))
      });
  }

  criterion_group!(benches, criterion_benchmark);
  criterion_main!(benches);
  ```

- **Employ profiling tools** to identify actual bottlenecks

  ```bash
  # Using cargo-flamegraph for visualization
  $ cargo install flamegraph
  $ cargo flamegraph --bin my_program

  # Using perf
  $ perf record -g ./target/release/my_program
  $ perf report

  # Using valgrind/callgrind
  $ valgrind --tool=callgrind ./target/release/my_program
  $ kcachegrind callgrind.out.*
  ```

- **Make data-driven optimization decisions**

  ```rust
  // Before optimization, measure performance
  fn optimize_process() {
      let data = load_test_data();

      // Measure baseline
      let start = std::time::Instant::now();
      let result1 = process_data_v1(&data);
      let duration1 = start.elapsed();
      println!("Version 1: {:?}", duration1);

      // Measure optimized version
      let start = std::time::Instant::now();
      let result2 = process_data_v2(&data);
      let duration2 = start.elapsed();
      println!("Version 2: {:?}", duration2);

      // Verify results are equivalent
      assert_eq!(result1, result2);

      // Calculate improvement
      let improvement = (duration1.as_micros() as f64) / (duration2.as_micros() as f64);
      println!("Speedup: {:.2}x", improvement);
  }
  ```

- **Document performance characteristics** and tradeoffs

  ```rust
  /// Finds the median value in the given slice.
  ///
  /// # Performance
  ///
  /// This implementation has O(n) average time complexity using
  /// quickselect algorithm, and O(1) space complexity.
  ///
  /// For sorted inputs, consider using the O(1) `sorted_median` function instead.
  pub fn median(data: &mut [f64]) -> Option<f64> {
      if data.is_empty() {
          return None;
      }

      let mid = data.len() / 2;
      quickselect(data, mid);

      Some(if data.len() % 2 == 1 {
          data[mid]
      } else {
          // For even lengths, average the two middle values
          (data[mid - 1] + data[mid]) / 2.0
      })
  }
  ```

## Utilize parallelism safely

- **Use `rayon` for data-parallel operations**

  ```rust
  use rayon::prelude::*;

  // Sequential processing
  fn sum_of_squares_sequential(input: &[i32]) -> i32 {
      input.iter()
          .map(|&x| x * x)
          .sum()
  }

  // Parallel processing with rayon
  fn sum_of_squares_parallel(input: &[i32]) -> i32 {
      input.par_iter()
          .map(|&x| x * x)
          .sum()
  }

  // Parallel processing with custom chunks
  fn process_chunks_parallel(data: &[u8]) -> Vec<u32> {
      data.par_chunks(1024)
          .map(|chunk| process_chunk(chunk))
          .collect()
  }

  fn process_chunk(chunk: &[u8]) -> u32 {
      // Process a single chunk...
      chunk.iter().map(|&b| b as u32).sum()
  }
  ```

- **Consider async/await for I/O-bound tasks**

  ```rust
  use tokio::fs::File;
  use tokio::io::{AsyncReadExt, AsyncWriteExt};

  #[tokio::main]
  async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
      // Process multiple files concurrently
      let handles = vec![
          tokio::spawn(process_file("file1.txt", "output1.txt")),
          tokio::spawn(process_file("file2.txt", "output2.txt")),
          tokio::spawn(process_file("file3.txt", "output3.txt")),
      ];

      // Wait for all to complete
      for handle in handles {
          handle.await??;
      }

      Ok(())
  }

  async fn process_file(input: &str, output: &str) -> Result<(), std::io::Error> {
      // Open input file
      let mut input_file = File::open(input).await?;

      // Read content
      let mut buffer = Vec::new();
      input_file.read_to_end(&mut buffer).await?;

      // Process (CPU-bound work could be offloaded to a thread pool)
      let processed = buffer.iter().map(|&b| b.to_ascii_uppercase()).collect::<Vec<_>>();

      // Write output
      let mut output_file = File::create(output).await?;
      output_file.write_all(&processed).await?;

      Ok(())
  }
  ```

- **Use thread pools for CPU-bound tasks**

  ```rust
  use std::sync::mpsc;
  use std::thread;

  // Simple thread pool implementation
  struct ThreadPool {
      workers: Vec<Worker>,
      sender: mpsc::Sender<Job>,
  }

  type Job = Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + 'static>;

  impl ThreadPool {
      fn new(size: usize) -> Self {
          assert!(size > 0);

          let (sender, receiver) = mpsc::channel();
          let receiver = std::sync::Arc::new(std::sync::Mutex::new(receiver));

          let mut workers = Vec::with_capacity(size);
          for id in 0..size {
              workers.push(Worker::new(id, std::sync::Arc::clone(&receiver)));
          }

          ThreadPool { workers, sender }
      }

      fn execute<F>(&self, f: F)
      where
          F: FnOnce() + Send + 'static,
      {
          let job = Box::new(f);
          self.sender.send(job).unwrap();
      }
  }

  struct Worker {
      id: usize,
      thread: Option<thread::JoinHandle<()>>,
  }

  impl Worker {
      fn new(id: usize, receiver: std::sync::Arc<std::sync::Mutex<mpsc::Receiver<Job>>>) -> Self {
          let thread = thread::spawn(move || loop {
              let job = receiver.lock().unwrap().recv().unwrap();
              println!("Worker {} got a job; executing.", id);
              job();
          });

          Worker {
              id,
              thread: Some(thread),
          }
      }
  }

  // Using the thread pool
  fn main() {
      let pool = ThreadPool::new(4);

      for i in 0..8 {
          pool.execute(move || {
              println!("Processing task {}", i);
              // CPU-intensive work here
              thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_secs(1));
          });
      }

      // Wait for some time to allow tasks to complete
      thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_secs(10));
  }
  ```

- **Employ lock-free data structures** when appropriate

  ```rust
  use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};
  use std::sync::Arc;
  use std::thread;

  // Lock-free counter using atomics
  struct Counter {
      count: AtomicUsize,
  }

  impl Counter {
      fn new() -> Self {
          Counter {
              count: AtomicUsize::new(0),
          }
      }

      fn increment(&self) {
          self.count.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);
      }

      fn value(&self) -> usize {
          self.count.load(Ordering::SeqCst)
      }
  }

  fn main() {
      let counter = Arc::new(Counter::new());
      let mut handles = vec![];

      // Spawn 10 threads to increment the counter
      for _ in 0..10 {
          let counter = Arc::clone(&counter);
          let handle = thread::spawn(move || {
              for _ in 0..1000 {
                  counter.increment();
              }
          });
          handles.push(handle);
      }

      // Wait for all threads to complete
      for handle in handles {
          handle.join().unwrap();
      }

      // Should print "Counter value: 10000"
      println!("Counter value: {}", counter.value());
  }
  ```

## Compile-time optimizations

- **Use appropriate compiler flags**

  ```toml
  # Cargo.toml
  [profile.release]
  # Enable link-time optimization
  lto = true
  # Maximum optimization level
  opt-level = 3
  # Codegen units (smaller values increase optimization but slow compilation)
  codegen-units = 1
  # Panic strategy (abort is smaller than unwind)
  panic = "abort"
  ```

- **Enable profile-guided optimization** for maximum performance

  ```bash
  # Step 1: Build with instrumentation
  $ RUSTFLAGS="-Cprofile-generate=/tmp/pgo-data" cargo build --release

  # Step 2: Run the program to collect profile data
  $ ./target/release/my_program

  # Step 3: Merge profile data
  $ llvm-profdata merge -o /tmp/pgo-data.profdata /tmp/pgo-data

  # Step 4: Build with profile data
  $ RUSTFLAGS="-Cprofile-use=/tmp/pgo-data.profdata" cargo build --release
  ```

- **Consider specialized builds** for different platforms

  ```toml
  # .cargo/config.toml

  # CPU-specific optimizations for your local machine
  [target.'cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")']
  rustflags = ["-C", "target-cpu=native"]

  # Optimize for different architectures
  [target.x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu]
  rustflags = ["-C", "target-feature=+avx2"]

  [target.wasm32-unknown-unknown]
  rustflags = ["-C", "opt-level=s"]  # Optimize for size
  ```

## Common mistakes

- **Premature optimization**: Optimizing before identifying actual bottlenecks
- **Benchmark bias**: Not accounting for warm-up, caching, or jitter in measurements
- **Unsafe without verification**: Using unsafe code without proper verification
- **Inappropriate parallelism**: Adding concurrency to non-bottleneck code, creating overhead
- **Algorithmic inefficiency**: Focusing on micro-optimizations instead of algorithm improvements
- **Memory fragmentation**: Frequent allocations and deallocations causing fragmentation
- **Cache thrashing**: Poor memory access patterns causing cache misses
- **False sharing**: Multiple threads accessing the same cache line causing contention
- **Over-abstraction**: Creating complex abstractions that prevent compiler optimizations
- **Lock contention**: Excessive locking causing threads to block frequently

